  "reasoning": "The product features a quaternary carbon center bonded to four distinct groups: an ethoxy group, an isopropyl group, a 4-chloro-4'-(4-chlorophenoxy)phenyl aryl group, and a pyrazole ring attached via its nitrogen atom. Under the reaction type 'Heteroatom alkylation and arylation', the standard retrosynthetic strategy is to disconnect the bond between the heteroatom nucleophile and the alkyl/aryl electrophile. Breaking the N–C bond between the pyrazole nitrogen and the central carbon cleanly separates the molecule into a pyrazole nucleophile and a tertiary alkyl chloride electrophile. This matches the pattern seen in reference examples where heterocycles are alkylated by halogenated alkyl chains. An alternative O-alkylation disconnection (yielding a tertiary alcohol and bromoethane) is chemically less favorable due to severe steric hindrance in synthesizing tertiary ethers. Therefore, the most logical precursors are pyrazole and the corresponding tertiary chloride bearing the remaining substituents.",
  "final_answer": "n1cncn1.CCOC(C(Cl)(c1ccc(Oc2ccc(Cl)cc2)cc1Cl)C(C)C"
}

